"""Substantive final-edition additions and updated code references."""
FINAL_SUPPLEMENTS={
1:[('Industrial automation: why an entire computer system mattered', '''A PDP-11 installation could serve as a data-collection station, a supervisory controller, a laboratory processor, or the host of a larger process-control system. These roles must not be conflated. In a data-acquisition station, measurements may be recorded for later decisions. A supervisory controller can propose setpoints for local controllers. In direct digital control, the computer participates more directly in choosing actuating commands. Each arrangement requires a different response to a lost sample, a broken cable, or a processor restart.

The DEC architecture made these configurations practical because the machine was not limited to one fixed peripheral arrangement. UNIBUS connected multiple devices in a consistent architectural framework; bus-mastering interfaces could move data efficiently; vectored interrupts allowed peripheral events to direct software attention. This is system architecture, not merely a fast instruction set. A laboratory temperature task does not require a UNIBUS, but it benefits from the same habit of defining a processor-to-device contract.

Use the historical rolling-mill case as an engineering design exercise. First separate measurements (thermocouple or pyrometer), identification (the strip section being measured), calculation (a limit and rate-of-change rule), and action (alarm or process-control request). Then add an independent protective layer for dangerous outcomes. Finally record which statements are historical capabilities and which are features of the classroom model. This discipline prevents an attractive analog from becoming an invented historical reconstruction.'''),
('PDP-11 hardware and the industrial interface contract', '''A CPU instruction that reads a device register is only the last link of a long measurement chain. Analog inputs require excitation or signal conditioning, conversion, appropriate reference voltages, grounding, and shielding. Digital contacts require defined polarity, isolation where required, and a known response to an open circuit. An external device may be slower than a CPU; software must account for settling, busy status, completion flags, and lost interrupts.

A useful classroom specification identifies, for each input, its physical variable, electrical representation, engineering units, normal range, valid-update interval, failure state, and timestamp origin. For each output it records the requested action, actuator interface, safe state, and independent limits. The PDP-11 hardware tradition was valuable because programmable logic and industrial interfaces could be integrated, not because software could ignore the plant. The MSP430 student should adopt exactly this interface-contract habit at a far smaller scale.''')],
3:[('RSX-11M as a real-time software case study', '''RSX-11M provided an operating environment for multiple tasks whose execution was related to events, priorities, and device activity. A useful mental model separates a rapidly serviced input from a periodic measurement computation and a relatively slow operator display. The highest-priority activity does not necessarily perform the most work; it handles the shortest deadline and passes compact information to other tasks.

Build a paper scheduling trace with three fictional jobs: an input alarm requiring a response within 2 ms, a measurement calculation released every 10 ms, and a status display released every 1 s. Add a 1 ms interval in which interrupts are disabled. The class must decide what can be concluded about worst-case alarm latency and what remains unknown about interrupt recognition, queueing, CPU service time, and actual hardware. There is no single correct schedule without these assumptions.

On an MSP430, the analogy can be explored with an interrupt service routine that copies an event flag or timestamp and returns, while foreground code handles reporting. This is not an implementation of RSX-11M and not equivalent to its task management. The historical point is that responsive industrial systems separate priority, latency, throughput, and noncritical reporting; the modern experiment puts measurable evidence under those abstractions.''')],
4:[('Instruction encoding laboratory: where did the bits go?', '''The PDP-11 used three bits for each register identifier and three bits for each operand addressing mode. Its regular double-operand format made both operand fields six bits. In the MSP430 Format I representation, a four-bit source register field and two source-mode bits coexist with a four-bit destination register field, only one destination-mode bit, an opcode field, and a byte/word bit. The teaching question is not whether the later CPU has more registers but how the architectural design spends the limited space in its sixteen-bit instruction word.

For a controlled experiment assemble `MOV.W R4,R5`, `MOV.W @R4+,R5`, `MOV.W #8,R5`, and `MOV.W #1234,R5` using one known MSP430 assembler. Preserve the compiler/assembler command and generated listing. Predict which instructions use a constant-generator encoding and which need an additional word. Because the operands occupy different places in the instruction field, the source register and addressing mode affect the instruction word even when the mnemonic is the same.

Then switch to the PDP-11 and inspect the source syntax for register, indexed, and autoincrement operations. The PDP-11 also uses the PC as an addressable register, so immediate operands can be understood as a special use of PC autoincrement. Similar-looking operands do not always have identical restrictions or encoding, and source and destination modes must be verified against the individual architecture manuals.''')],
7:[('A concrete deadline budget', '''Consider an event at an MSP430 input. Before useful application code executes, the signal must reach the pin and meet electrical requirements, the interrupt mechanism must recognize it, the CPU must complete or interrupt its current activity as defined by the architecture, and context must be saved. The handler then performs instructions and may wake lower-priority code. If a handler toggles a measurement pin only after inspecting a flag, that inspection is part of the observed latency.

Write a budget using illustrative numbers: input recognition 5 microseconds, entry and context 8 microseconds, handler work 20 microseconds, and exit or handoff 7 microseconds. The nominal sum is 40 microseconds. It is only a paper model: real intervals must be obtained from the target clock, interrupt settings, compiler-generated code, and a timing instrument. If another operation can postpone interrupts by 70 microseconds, the model must include that delay in its worst-case bound.

Students should capture at least twenty time intervals rather than reporting a single convenient one. Record not only an average but minimum, maximum, the number of samples, and the instrument time resolution. A measured maximum over twenty events is evidence about those events; it is not a proof of the absolute worst case under all legal operating conditions.''')],
9:[('Engineering the one-wire port rather than copying a delay loop', '''Start with the electrical rule: a bus master must pull DQ low or release it. The master should never drive DQ actively high when the sensor may also drive it low. For the proposed MSP430 board the candidate DQ net is P1.7; inspect the physical pull-up and remove other active devices from that shared I/O line before applying voltage. Keep sensor VDD connected to 3.3 V for the introductory project; parasite-power mode introduces different power-delivery conditions.

The candidate 1-Wire transport uses Timer_A0 in continuous mode as a nominal microsecond counter. That imposes a real resource conflict: it cannot simultaneously use the same timer configuration as the earlier Timer_A0 1 kHz exercise. The exercises are intentionally separate firmware images. An engineer may later choose another timer or a dedicated PIO probe, but must document the revised ownership and routing.

For a write-one slot, a short low pulse is followed by release; for a write-zero slot, the low interval occupies most of the slot. A read slot begins with a low pulse, a release, and a sample taken during the device's defined response window. Show those phases as separate regions on a measured waveform. A logic analyzer used at an inadequate sample rate can make a nominal 6-microsecond pulse appear to have different widths, so characterize the capture instrument too. Bus capacitance, the resistor, wiring length, and the sensor's input characteristics all affect rise time.

Before trusting a temperature transaction, demand a reset-low capture, a presence response with plausible timing, a write-one and write-zero capture, and a read slot with documented sample location. Repeat after changing only the clock setting, then explain why a fixed software delay can fail when the DCO changes. Do not interpret a correct 1 kHz frequency reading as proof that these microsecond slots satisfy the DS18B20 datasheet.''')],
10:[('Worked complete sensor transaction: commands, waiting, and validity', '''A transaction must represent the *whole* physical operation. After an idle-high check, issue a reset and look for a genuine presence pulse. With exactly one known sensor on the bus, the Skip ROM command (0xCC) can be used, followed by Convert T (0x44). The simplified candidate waits the maximum 750 ms for a 12-bit conversion with the sensor externally powered. Reset again, select the one sensor, issue Read Scratchpad (0xBE), and collect all nine bytes. Check the CRC of bytes zero through seven against byte eight and then decode the signed raw temperature value.

This sequence deliberately makes the limitations visible. A successful CRC does not prove that the convert command was actually completed, because old or power-up scratchpad data can be returned. A result of 85 °C can be a legitimate physical measurement or the DS18B20's initial register representation; the software must establish that a new conversion was commanded and allowed to finish. Missing presence, CRC failure, unexpected sensor count, and stale data are separate fault categories that deserve distinct status codes and records.

The companion `10_ds18b20_transaction_CANDIDATE.c` is deliberately blocking and limited to one externally powered sensor. Its delay assumes an appropriate nominal 1 MHz MCU clock, and its transport functions are imported from the unvalidated 1-Wire module. A real product would introduce a nonblocking schedule and a sensor identity check. The teaching version offers an inspectable transaction before layering on an event-driven application.''')],
11:[('A supervisory system is more than a thermostat', '''Consider a simulated industrial supervisor that reads temperature, chooses a cooling-demand state, announces it over UART, and uses LEDs to expose faults. A cooling request becomes ON at 30 °C or above and OFF at 28 °C or below. Between those boundaries, the last valid state persists. If the sensor is invalid, the teaching policy requests the FAULT state, with the active output disabled. Upon the next valid sample inside the hysteresis band, this implementation returns to OFF rather than silently restoring the pre-fault ON command.

That policy is suitable only for a harmless classroom LED. Whether an actual industrial plant should open a valve, close a valve, stop a pump, or run an emergency fan upon sensor failure cannot be decided by a generic C function. It depends on hazard analysis, actuator polarity, independent interlocks, and the cost of both false positive and false negative action. Students should write a process-specific safe-state rationale before proposing anything stronger than an LED.

The included `11_supervisor_demo_HOST.c` runs a sequence of eight synthetic measurements and prints a CSV-compatible state trace. Its 1/16-degree integer arithmetic is the same representation used by the DS18B20 decoding code. The trace establishes the decision function's behavior on those cases but gives no evidence about any attached sensor or output hardware. Challenge the student to add a test case for exactly 28 °C, exactly 30 °C, a loss of sensor validity, and recovery with a new valid sample. Verify them on the host before porting to a target.''')],
12:[('A repeatable experiment includes a software bill of materials', '''A source archive without versions or run instructions is difficult to reuse, particularly after a reboot or long gap between sessions. Every published experiment should include a short manifest: target device, PCB revision, toolchain and linker configuration, source commit or archive checksum, programming procedure, I/O wiring, instrument firmware, measurement settings, and raw output. Include an environment note stating what was not available or tested.

For the software validation exercise, run `tests/run_host_tests.sh` on a computer with Python and a C compiler. The script tests the CSV summarizer, scratchpad CRC and signed decoding, the hysteresis state machine, and a human-readable host demonstration. A passing host test validates those functions under the tested inputs, not a complete microcontroller program. An appropriate publication includes both the passing log and the separate target-compilation and physical test records when those are eventually obtained.

For a publication-ready figure, label each axis with units, distinguish measured from computed values, show actual sample count, identify the independent timing reference if one exists, and discuss the limits imposed by count quantization and timebase drift. A neat graph that omits its reference clock is less informative than a simple table that records every raw count and timestamp.''')]
}
for key,items in FINAL_SUPPLEMENTS.items():
    SUPPLEMENTS.setdefault(key,[]).extend(items)
CODE_MAP[10].append('msp430/10_ds18b20_transaction_CANDIDATE.c')
CODE_MAP[11].append('msp430/11_supervisor_demo_HOST.c')
